WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.579
Three splits, three jobs.

2
00:00:01.859 --> 00:00:04.333
The training set is what the model learns from.

3
00:00:04.613 --> 00:00:14.384
The validation set (also called the dev set) is what you use to make decisions: how many epochs, which learning rate, which checkpoint, whether a data change helped.

4
00:00:14.664 --> 00:00:25.053
Gekhman and colleagues’ new-knowledge result from lesson 4 depended on it: stopping when validation accuracy peaked avoided most of the harm from training too long.

5
00:00:25.333 --> 00:00:29.493
The test set is what you report, ideally once per decision you’re making.

6
00:00:29.773 --> 00:00:35.000
The reason for separating validation from test is a statistical fact you already know.

7
00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:42.768
If you measure ten checkpoints on the same set and pick the best, the best score is biased upward: you selected partly on noise.

8
00:00:43.048 --> 00:00:47.293
The more choices you make on a set, the more optimistic its numbers become.

9
00:00:47.573 --> 00:00:51.285
The validation set absorbs that bias so the test set doesn’t.

10
00:00:51.565 --> 00:01:00.717
Hamel Husain and Shreya Shankar’s evals FAQ applies the same rule to LLM judges: tune on one split, measure on another.

11
00:01:00.997 --> 00:01:02.341
How big should each be?

12
00:01:02.621 --> 00:01:07.912
The test set must be large enough that the differences you care about exceed its margin of error.

13
00:01:08.192 --> 00:01:17.557
In Studio 3 you computed that 50 cases at 80 percent give roughly plus or minus 11 points, which can’t separate 80 from 85.

14
00:01:17.837 --> 00:01:28.696
For fine-tuning, a common pattern is to fix the test set’s size from that calculation first, set aside a similar or smaller validation set, and give everything else to training.

15
00:01:28.976 --> 00:01:39.728
The lab’s 70/15/15 split of 44 documents is far too small for a real benchmark, and saying so out loud is part of the exercise.
